    "ls|LS16_S14_U_610": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING AND FISHERIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.610 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6TH FEBRUARY, 2018 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF INLAND FISHERIES AND AQUACULTURE \n \n 610.   SHRI BALABHADRA MAJHI:                    \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि और ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has allocated sufficient funds under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme \n“Development of Inland Fisheries and Aquaculture” as per requirement of the State, if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government is considering to revise the unit cost as well as the corresponding subsidy \nthereon taking the scheduled rate and prevailing market rate; and  \n \n(c) whether the Government is likely to give instructions to insurance companies to insure the fish ponds \nof fishermen, if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \n(SHRIMATI  KRISHNA RAJ) \n \n(a):    The Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare, Department of Animal Husbandry, Dairying \n& Fisheries is implementing a Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) ‘Blue Revolution: Integrated \nDevelopment and Management of Fisheries’ with a component on “Deve lopment of Inland Fisheries \nand Aquaculture” for development of fisheries sector in the country. A statement showing the State-wise \ndetails of tentative funds allocated under the CSS during the current financial year (2017-18) is annexed.  \n \n(b)&(c): No such proposals are pending in this Ministry.  \n \n \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_611": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 611 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06/02/2018 \n \nLOSSES OF FARM PRODUCE DUE TO WEEDS \n \n611. SHRI RATTAN LAL KATARIA: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether according to a survey conducted by the Indian Council of Agricultural \nResearch (ICAR) and the Research Association of India, the 10 major crops s uffer huge \neconomic losses due to weeds every year in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) the efforts are being made by the Government to tackle the menace of weed so that \nthese losses may be reduced substantially; and \n \n(c) whether it is fact that weeds are responsible for decreasing production of eight most \nimportant food and cash crops by 13.6 per cent globally and if so, the details thereof? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f”k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n \n(a) Yes, Madam.  A study was conducted to estimate the yield and economic losses due \nto weeds using the data from 1581 On -Farm Research trials conducted by All India \nCoordinated Research Pro ject (AICRP) on Weed Management between 2003 - 2014 in 10 \nmajor field crops in different districts of 18 states of India. The economic losses due to \nweeds alone in 10 major crops of India were highest in rice followed by wheat and soybean. \nThe details are given in Annexure-I. \n  \n(b) ICAR through DWR, Jabalpur, AICRP on Weed Management, other institutes, \nKVK’s and State Department s of Agriculture is providing technical \nadvisory/recommendations on weed management in major crops. ICAR -DWR and AICRP- \nWeed Management centres are also conducting on -farm research trial/field demonstration, \nFarmers’ fair, Farmer -Scientist interactions, Kisan Sangosthi and capacity building \nprogrammes to create awareness among farmers and other stakeholders to adopt improved \nweed management technologies in different crops to reduce/minimize economic losses due \nto weeds. The developed/validated weed management practices are also included in \npackage of practices of respective states to tackle the menace of weeds. \n \n(c) Weeds are responsibl e for decreasing the production of most important food and \ncash crops by 13.2%. The details are given in Annexure-II. \n******* \n\n2 \n \n \nAnnexure-I \n[Part (a) of Lok Sabha USQ No. 611 for 06/02/2018] \n \nEconomic losses due to weeds \n \n \nCrop Losses (in US$ in millions) \nRice 4420 \nSoybean 1559 \nGroundnut 283 \nPearlmillet 17 \nSorghum 276 \nGreengram 161 \nSesame 50 \nWheat 3376 \nMaize 739 \nMustard 72 \n \nActual losses due to weeds in major Indian crops \n \n \nCrop Actual losses due to weeds (%) \nWheat 18.6 \nRice (transplanted) 13.8 \nRice (direct-seeded) 21.4 \nMaize 25.3 \nGroundnut  35.8 \nSoybean 31.4 \nGreen gram 30.8 \nSesame  23.7 \nPearlmillet  27.6 \nMustard  21.4 \nSorghum 25.1 \n \n \n****** \n \n\n3 \n \nAnnexure-II \n[Part (c) of Lok Sabha USQ No. 611 for 06/02/2018] \n \nReferences indicating decreasing production of important food and cash crops due to \nweeds: \n \n1. Oerke, E.C., 2006. Crop losses to pests: centenary review. Journal of Agricultural \nScience 144, 31–43. \n2. Sardana, V., Mahaja, G, Jabran, K. and Chauhan B.S. 2017. Role of competition \nin managing weeds; an introduction to the special issue. Crop Protection 95: 1-7. \n3. Yaduraju, N.T., 2012. Weed management perspectives for India in the changing \nagriculture scenario in the country. Pakistan Journal of Weed Science Research \n18: 703–710. \n****** ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_612": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 612     \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06TH FEBRUARY, 2018 \n \nFOOD SECURITY EFFORTS AND HUNGER \n \n612. SHRI INNOCENT: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether it is true that 15 per cent  of the Indian population do not get \na full meal daily and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether it is also true that the food security efforts made by the \nGovernment have not achieved the desired results; and \n \n(c)  if so, the measures taken by the Government to review the \nimplementation of food security schemes?  \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n(a) to (c) –  With a view to providing food security, Government of India \nenacted National Food Security Act (NFSA) 2013, which provides for \ncoverage of upto 75% of the rural population and upto 50% of the urban \npopulation for receiving subsidized foodgrains under Targeted Public \nDistribution System (TPDS), covering about two- third of the total population \nof the country. The eligible persons identified by the States/UTs are provided \nfoodgrains @ 35kg per family per month for AAY category and @ 5 kg per \nperson per month for PHH category at highly subsidized prices of Re.1/ -, \nRs.2/- and Rs.3/- per kg for coarse grains, wheat and rice respectively. 80.72 \ncrore persons are covered against the ceiling of 81.34 crore provided in the \nNFSA. \n \n  The Act also has a special focus on nutritional support to women and \nchildren. Pregnant women and lactating mothers are entitled to receive \nmeals as per nutritional norms. Children upto 14 years of age are also \nentitled to nutritious meals or take home rations as per the prescribed \nnutritional standards.  In case of non supply of entitled foodgrains or meals, \nthe beneficiaries are entitled to receive food security allowance as per \nNFSA, 2013. \n \n  The implementation of the Act is reviewed regularly at various levels. \nNFSA provides for State Food Commission, District Grievance Redressal \nOfficers, Vigilance Committees at the Fair Price Shop (FPS), Block, District \nand State levels and social audit. Central Govt. reviews the implementation \nof the Act through review meetings, video-conferences, field visits, etc. \n****  ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_613": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING AND FISHERIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.613 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6TH FEBRUARY, 2018 \n \nBAN ON CATFISH \n \n613:   SHRI PONGULETI SRINIVASA REDDY: \n   \n         Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ षि और ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that in many parts of southern State in the country including Telangana the \nbanned catfish variety is still being reared/cultivated which is very dangerous to other fish species;  \n \n(b)    if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether it is  true that the Government has banned the said catfish variety in a bid to save other \nvarieties of fish in India but the same catfish variety is being imported from Bangladesh in an \nunscrupulous manner; and \n \n (d)  if so, the measures taken by the Government  to strictly ban the said catfish variety in the country \nparticularly in Telangana? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \n(SHRIMATI  KRISHNA RAJ) \n \n(a)  Yes, Madam.  \n \n(b)  Reports indicate that the exotic catfish ( Clarias gariepinus) also known as Thai \nmagur/African catfish is being cultured in limited parts of southern States like Karnataka despite efforts \nmade by the State Government in restraining the fish farmers from culturing this species. \n \n(c)&(d)  Based on the recom mendations of the National Committee on Introduction of Exotic \nAquatic Species into Indian Waters, the State Governments were advised in the year 1997 to take \nnecessary steps to prevent culture of exotic magur ( Clarias gariepinus), which had been introduce d in \nthe country without official sanction. However, no reports have been received regarding unscrupulous \nimport of this species from Bangladesh or elsewhere. \n \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_614": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 614 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 06, 2018 \nSMART CITY MISSION IN NORTH EASTERN REGION \nNo. 614   SHRI NINONG ERING: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the current status of the Smart City Mission in the North \nEastern Region and the details of works undertaken and likely \nto be undertaken in the Region; \n \n(b) whether steps have been taken for the protection and \npreservation of the water bodi es, as well as, indigenous and \nendangered flora and fauna under the said mission;    \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof during the last three years, State-wise; \nand  \n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \n \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a): Under the Smart Cities Mi ssion (SCM), nine cities from \nthe North Eastern Region have been selected in four \nRounds. Guwahati in Assam was selected in Round 1 in \nJanuary 2016; Agartala in Tripura and Imphal in Manipur \nwere selected in fast track round in May 2016; Kohima in \nNagaland and Namchi in Sikkim were selected in the \nRound 2 in September 2016; Aizawl in Mizoram, Gangtok \nin Sikkim, and Pasighat in Arunachal Pradesh were \n\nselected in the Round 3 in June 2017; Itanagar in \nArunachal Pradesh has recently been selected in Round \n4 in January 2018. \nGuwahati, Agartala, Imphal, Kohima, Namchi and \nPasighat have incorporated Special Purpose Vehicles \n(SPVs) for implementation of Mission at city level. The \nremaining cities are in the process of incorporation of \nSPVs. Project Management Consultants (PMCs) have \nbeen engaged in Guwahati, Namchi and Agartala for \nprojectivization of projects. \nThe details of proposed projects to be undertaken in \nthese cities are given in the Smart City Proposals (SCPs) \nwhich are available on Mission’s website \n(www.smartcities.gov.in).  \n(b) to (d): Under Smart Cities  Mission (SCM), there has been a \nfocus on innovative use of open spaces including \nwaterfronts including abetment of pollution in water \nbodies and conserving/ restoring the natural \nenvironment. Some of the projects proposed by Smart \nCities located in the North East are as follows: \nSl. \nNo. \n \nCity  \n \nProjects \n \n1 Guwahati Borsola Beel (Lake) \n2 Guwahati Mora Bharalu River \n3 Guwahati Bharalu River \n4 Guwahati Brahmaputra River Front \nDevelopment \n5 Guwahati Deepor Beel (Lake/Wetland) \n6 Imphal West Riverfront & Landscaped  \nGreen  \n7 Imphal East Riverfront & Landscaped \nGreen \n8 Agartala Katakal Channel Rejuvenation \n9 Agartala MBB College Lake Revitalization \n10 Agartala Innovative use of Water Bodies \n11 Agartala  Development and beautification \nof Park and Open spaces around \n\nSl. \nNo. \n \nCity  \n \nProjects \n \nSath Lakhi Pond \n12 Agartala Improvement and Beautification \nof the Bodhjung Girls School \nPond \n13 Agartala Improvement and beautification \nof Water Body at Kamar Pukur \nPond \n14 Pasighat Bund road and new road \nconstruction  \n15 Pasighat Silt dredging \n16 Pasighat Illumination along river bank  \n17 Pasighat River deck  \n18 Pasighat Riverfront Landscaping  \n \n \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_615": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 615 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH FEBRUARY, 2018/ MAGHA 17, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nBHARAT RATNA FOR FIELD MARSHAL CARIAPPA  \n \n615.  SHRI RAJAN VICHARE: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to award ‘Bharat Ratna’, the \nhighest Civilian Award, Posthumously to Field Marshal K.M. Cariappa , one \nof the five stars General of the Indian Armed forces; and  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) & (b): The decisions regarding award of Bharat Ratna  are taken from \ntime to time.  No formal recommendations for this are necessary. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_616": "                                                                                                                      \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.616  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 6TH FEBRUARY, 2018 \n \nFOODGRAIN PRODUCTION \n \n616.  DR. KIRIT P. SOLANKI: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   कृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of foodgrain production  including vari ous pulses, cereals and rice in the \ncountry during the current year; \n \n(b)  the estimated rate of growth of the agriculture sector during the said period; \n \n(c)  whether the foodgrain output has  declined/ is likely to decline during the Kharif season \nand if so, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d)  the steps taken by the Government  to increase foodgrain output and boost the growth \nrate of the agriculture sector? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nकृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्राऱय में राज्य मंत्री     (SHRI  GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n \n(a): It is early to have a firm assessment of increase or decrease of total production of pulses \nin 2017-18 for the current year as the Second Advance estimate covering b oth Kharif and Rabi \npulses are normally released in mid-February. \n \nThe details of kharif foodgrain production (as per 1 st Advance Estimate 2017 -18) \nincluding various pulses, cereals and rice in the country during the current year is given as \nunder: \n                                                                                           (in Million tonnes) \nCrop Production \nRice 94.48 \nTotal Cereals 125.96 \nTur 3.99 \nUrad 2.53 \nMoong 1.32 \nTotal Pulses 8.71 \nTotal Foodgrains 134.67 \n \n Contd…2/- \n \n\n-2- \n \n \n(b):  As per First Advance Estimates of National Income 2017 -18 released by Ministry of \nStatistics and Programme Implementation, the rate of growth of agriculture & allied sector \nduring the year 2017-18 is estimated at 2.1%. \n \n(c):  Total production of Kharif F oodgrains during 2017 -18 is estimated at 134.67 million \ntonnes (as per first advance estimate) which is lower by 3.86 million tonnes as compared to last \nyear’s Kharif foodgrains record production of 138.52 million tonnes (as per fourth advance \nestimate). H owever, estimated kharif foodgrain production of 2017 -18 is 6.43 million tonnes \nmore than the average production of five years ending 2015-16. \n \nFoodgrain output varies and exhibits a mixed trend on year to year basis depending on \nvariations in weather, rai nfall situation, irrigation facilities and shift in the area due to other \ncompeting crops. \n \n(d): To increase the foodgrain production and  to boost the growth rate of the agriculture sector \nGovernment implements various schemes such a s Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana , National \nFood Security Mission, Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture, Soil Health Card, \npromotion of Neem Coated Urea, Pradh an Mantri Krishi Sinchai Yojana, Pradhan Mantri Fasal \nBima Yojana , Paramparagat Krish i Vikas Yojana . Also, Price Support Scheme and Market \nIntervention Scheme are implemented to ensure remunerative prices to farmers. \n \n********  ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_618": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n618\n                     \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n6\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n, 201\n8\n \n \n              \nCONTRACT FARMING\n \n \n618. \n \nSHRI CH. MALLA REDDY:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has come\n \nout with a draft model for contract farming\n \nto \nprotect farmers from price \nrisks and\n \nencourage food processing companies to\n \ninvest \nmore in infrastructure and farming\n \ntechnology; and\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं \n\u0000कसान \nक\u0000याण \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nGAJENDRA \nSINGH SHEKHAWAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \nIn order to \nmitigate market and price risk and provide production support \nthrough supply of quality inputs and farming technology, Government is formulating a \nprogressive, holistic and facilitative model  contract farming Act, which is in the final \nstage  of its draft\ning.  Under the draft model Act, provisions have been made \ninter\n-\nalia \nfor\n \nintegrating food processors with farmers with an aim to ensure better price \nrealization to the farmers for their produce besides reducing post\n-\nharvest losses, and \nincentivizing the p\nrivate sector processors to invest in processing activities. \n \n \nComments of various stakeholders have been solicited on the draft model \nContract Farming Act including placing it on the website inviting comments of the public. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n*******\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_619": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS   \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 619 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH FEBRUARY, 2018/ MAGHA 17, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nDEATH OF PRISONERS  \n \n619.   SHRI VENKATESH BABU T.G. : \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that as per the National Crime \nRecords Bureau (NCRB), 552 prisoners died due to unnatural causes in the \ncountry between 2012 to 2015;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof, year-wise/ State-wise;  \n \n(c) whether inhuman conditions and lack of basic facilities in prisons has \nresulted in such a huge number of unnatural deaths;  \n \n(d) if so, whether the Government has issued any direction to the States to \nimprove the conditions in jails; and  \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n(a) to (e):  State/UT wise details of natural and unnatural death of prison \ninmates for the years 2012, 2013, 2014, and 2015 are given at  Annex  I.   \n \n“Prisons” is a State subject as per entry 4 of List II of the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India. The administration and management \nof prisons is primari ly the responsibility of respective State Governments.  \nHowever, the Government of India has been providing regular guidance and \nadvice to the States and UTs in the matter of prison administration and \nmanagement. The guidelines/advisories issued by MHA are  available at \nMHA’s website http://mha1.nic.in/PrisonReforms/advisiory.html. The \nMinistry of Home Affairs had also prepared a Model Prison Manual in 2016 \nand had forwarded it to all States/UTs .  The Manual provides detailed \nguidance to the States on maintenance of prisoners, medical care, welfare \nof prisoners, prison discipline, and women prisoners etc.  \n***** \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \nL.S.US.Q.NO.619 FOR 06.02.2018 \n