    "ls|LS16_S14_U_688": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEAUTICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 688 \n TO BE ANSWERED ON THE 6TH February, 2018  \n \nNIPER in Andhra Pradesh  \n \n688.  SHRI JAYADEV GALLA: \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to set up National Institute of \nPharmaceutical Education and Research (NIPER) in Andhra Pradesh; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)  the extent to which the NIPER helps in reducing the  demand for experts in \npharmacy and also make AP  a pharmaceutical hub?  \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND \nHIGHWAYS; MINISTRY OF SHIPPING AND MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND \nFERTILIZERS (SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) & (b): No, Madam. There is no such proposal  to set up National Institute of \nPharmaceutical Education and Research (NIPER) in Andhra Pradesh.  \n \n(c): At present seven National Institutes of Pharmaceutical Education and Research \n(NIPERs) are functioning at Mohali, Ahmedabad, Hyderabad, Guwahati, Raebareli, \nHajipur and Kolkata under the aegis of Department of Pharmaceuticals, Ministry of \nChemicals & Ferti lizers, Government of India. The basic objectives of setting up \nthese institutes are to nurture and promote quality and excellence in pharmaceutical \neducation and research. Since inception of these Institutes, 3391 students have \nbeen awarded postgraduates and 264 students doctorate degrees. These highly \nskilled trained manpower are contributing their knowledge to the national economy. \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_689": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 689  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6TH FEBRUARY, 2018 \n \nPradhan Mantri Bhartiya Jan Aushadhi Pariyojana \n \n689. SHRI DINESH TRIVEDI: \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the percentage of medical expenses in rural and urban areas spent on medicines; \n(b) the performance of the Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Jan Aushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP) \nscheme so far in providing generic drugs to poor people in the country; \n(c) whether the number of Jan Aushadhi Stores (JAS) are adequate as compared to \nthe large network of private pharmacies; \n(d) whether there is adequate stock/availability of medicines and sales in the JAS \nstores across the country, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \nand \n(e) whether the Government has any proposal go give licence to private pharmacies \nand online retailers such as Amazon and Flipkart to sell PMBJP medicines in order \nto enlarge the distribution network? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS; \nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING AND MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n                                  (SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) As on 31.01.2018, 3,096 ‘Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana \n(PMBJP) Kendras’ are functional in 33 States / Union Territories of the country \ncovering rural and urban areas of the country. However, the information regarding \npercentage of medical expenses spent on medicines in ru ral and urban areas, is \nnot maintained. \n(b) As on 31.01.2018, 3,096 ‘Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana \n(PMBJP) Kendras ’ are functional in 33 States / Union Territories of the country. \nThis has resulted in substantial savings to poor people in the area of healthcare. An \nillustrative list comparing the prices of generic medicines under PMBJP and \nbranded medicines is as under:   \nS. \nNo. \nMedicine Name Average Market \nPrice of top \nthree branded \nmedicines (Rs.) \nPMBJP’s \nPrice \n(Rs.) \n% \nSavings \n1 Amlodipine 5mg 10 Tablets 20 3.24 83.80 \n2 Atenolol 50 mg 14 Tablets 22 5.56 74.72 \n3 Clomiphene Citrate 50 mg 10 \nTablets \n85.10 15.04 82.32 \n4 Losartan 25 mg 10 Tablets 26 5.74 77.92 \n5 Metoprolol 50 mg 10 Tablets 53 5.95 88.77 \n \n\n(c) As on 31.01.2018, 3,096 ‘Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana \n(PMBJP) Kendras’ are functional in 33 States / Union Territories of the country. The \nGovernment approves all the eligible applications which are received for opening of \nPMBJP Kendras.  \n \n(d) Presently, the product basket of the scheme covers more than 700 medicines and \n154 su rgicals and consumables covering all major therapeutic groups. As on \n31.01.2018, 648 medicines and 79 surgicals & consumables are presently available \nin Central Ware House (CWH) of Bureau of Pharma PSUs of India (BPPI) (the \nimplementing agency of PMBJP) fo r supplying to PMBJP Kendras across the \ncountry. As on 31.01.2018, cumulative primary sales of the scheme has crossed \nRupees 106 Crores at Maximum Retail Price (MRP) terms in the current financial \nyear. \n \n(e) No Madam. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_690": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 690 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH FEBRUARY, 2018/ MAGHA 17, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nNGOs REGISTERED UNDER FCRA  \n \n690.  ADV. JOICE GEORGE: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the total number of Non Governmental Organisations (NGOs) registered \nunder the Foreign Contributions Regulation Act (FCRA);  \n \n(b) whether the Government has directed all NGOs, individuals and \nbusiness entities receiving foreign funds to open accounts in any of 32 \ndesignated banks, including one foreign account within a fixed time -frame \nrecently and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether these accounts will be integrated with Central Government’s \nPublic Financial Management System (PFMS) and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d) whether this will provide higher level of transparency and hassle -free \nreporting/compliance of the said d irection and if so, the details thereof; \nand  \n \n(e) the number of registration of NGOs cancelled by the Government since \n2014? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a): Approximately 29700 Non Governmental Organisations (NGOs) are \nregistered under The Foreign Contributions (Regulation) Act, 2010. \n \n \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO.690 FOR 06.02.2018 \n(b) to (d): Yes, Madam. Vide Notice dated 21.12.2017, the Government has \ndirected that all persons who are either registered or who have sought \nprior permission under the FCRA, 2010 shall open their bank accounts as \nmandated in section 17 of the FCRA, 2010 in any of the 32 banks who are \nalready integrated with the Public Financial Management System (PFMS) \nfor providing a higher level of transparency and hassle -free reporting \ncompliance. \n(e): The registration of 14,938 NGOs have been cancelled since 2014. \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_691": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.691 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY FEBRUARY 7, 2018 \n \nMONSOON DEFICIT DISTRICTS \n \n691.  SHRI JAYADEV GALLA: \n \nWill the Minister of EARTH SCIENCES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it has come to the notice of his Ministry that almost 235 districts \nin the country’s 630 districts face monsoon deficit of 20 per cent or more;  \n(b) if so, the details of districts so affected, State-wise; and  \n(c) the factors that are responsible for deficit rainfall and the manner in which \nthe Government is planning to address this problem?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY AND \nMINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES  \n(SHRI Y. S. CHOWDARY) \n \n(a) Yes Madam. However, the number of districts which faced deficient \nrainfall during the 2017 monsoon season are 215 out of a total number of \n660 districts.  \n \n(b) A list of State -wise districts with deficient  rainfall (-20% to -99%) is given \nin Annexure. \n \n(c) The rainfall activ ity during the monsoon season depends upon the \nformation of weather systems within the monsoon current.  The monsoon \nflow pattern had been comparatively weaker in the second half of the \nmonsoon season 2017 and the number of low pressure areas formed during \nAugust & September, 2017 had been less than the normal number \nexpected.  This has contributed towards the less than Long Period Average \n(LPA) rainfall for the country as a whole during August & September (87% \nof the LPA only) which has reflected in the di strict rainfall scenario also.  \nHowever, IMD is issuing five days weather based district level agromet \nadvisories to about 24 million registered farmers twice in a week to help \nfarmers in their day to day farming operations.  \n\nAnnexure \nLIST OF DISTRICTS WITH RAINFALL DEPARTURES: -20% TO -99% \nPeriod: (June 1 To September 30), 2017 \nS. \nNO. \nNAME OF DISTRICTS  \n  \n%  